To find the number of collisions per second, use the relation  

\[
\text{collisions per second} = \frac{v}{\lambda}
\]

where \(v = 1.21\times10^{6}\ \text{m/s}\) and \(\lambda = 4\times10^{-8}\ \text{m}\).

\[
\frac{1.21\times10^{6}}{4\times10^{-8}} = \frac{1.21}{4}\times10^{6+8}
= 0.3025\times10^{14} = 3.025\times10^{13}\ \text{s}^{-1}
\]

So about \(3.0\times10^{13}\) collisions per second.

The time between collisions is the mean free path divided by speed:

\[
\tau = \frac{\lambda}{v} = \frac{4\times10^{-8}}{1.21\times10^{6}}
= \frac{4}{1.21}\times10^{-14}
\approx 3.31\times10^{-14}\ \text{s}
\]

Rounded to two significant figures, \(\tau \approx 3.3\times10^{-14}\) s.

These values match option **(H)**.

**The answer is (H)**
